1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,229
Penny just walked out of a pawn shop with a hundred and fifty dollars.

2
00:00:04,070 --> 00:00:05,370
The pawnbroker is thrilled.

3
00:00:05,839 --> 00:00:07,129
And here's the strange part:

4
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:10,189
he's actually hoping she comes back to pay it off.

5
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:17,530
Most people think pawn shops make money by buying your stuff cheap and selling it high.

6
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:20,960
That's part of it.

7
00:00:21,899 --> 00:00:25,429
But the real engine is something most customers never notice.

8
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:29,070
By the end of this video, you'll know exactly how the machine works, and how not to get caught in it.

9
00:00:29,739 --> 00:00:31,089
Rewind a week.

10
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:33,460
Penny's car breaks down. The bill:

11
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:36,320
four hundred dollars. Her bank account:

12
00:00:37,079 --> 00:00:39,539
sixty-two.

13
00:00:40,320 --> 00:00:42,289
No credit card, no time for a bank loan.

14
00:00:42,840 --> 00:00:45,579
So she does what millions of people do:

15
00:00:46,439 --> 00:00:47,640
she grabs something valuable and heads to a pawn shop.

16
00:00:48,310 --> 00:00:50,570
She has two choices.

17
00:00:51,009 --> 00:00:54,039
Sell the guitar outright, or pawn it, which means using it as collateral for a short-term loan.

18
00:00:54,850 --> 00:00:57,479
She pawns it. She's getting it back, right?

19
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:07,709
First trick: the loan is only a fraction of what the item is worth, usually somewhere between a quarter and sixty percent of what the shop could resell it for.

20
00:01:08,640 --> 00:01:10,000
That gap isn't greed.

21
00:01:10,390 --> 00:01:16,189
It's insurance. If Penny never comes back, the shop still makes a profit selling the guitar.

22
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,180
But the shop would actually rather she came back.

23
00:01:19,799 --> 00:01:23,969
Here's why. Pawn loans don't charge interest the way a bank does.

24
00:01:24,579 --> 00:01:36,290
They charge monthly fees, and depending on where you live, those fees can run from two percent all the way up to twenty-five percent of the loan every single month.

25
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,459
Let's run Penny's numbers.

26
00:01:40,189 --> 00:01:43,930
Say her shop charges twenty percent a month. That's thirty dollars, every month, just to keep the loan alive.

27
00:01:44,739 --> 00:01:50,200
At the end of month one, she can't pay back the full one-eighty.

28
00:01:50,689 --> 00:01:52,280
No problem, says the shop.

29
00:01:52,599 --> 00:01:55,040
Just pay the thirty-dollar fee, and we'll extend it.

30
00:01:55,739 --> 00:01:57,700
So she does. Then again.

31
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:01,599
Then again.

32
00:02:01,980 --> 00:02:04,200
Three months later, she's paid ninety dollars in fees and she still owes the original one-fifty.

33
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,349
Her guitar hasn't moved.

34
00:02:07,099 --> 00:02:11,530
Even a ten percent monthly fee works out to a hundred and twenty percent a year.

35
00:02:12,569 --> 00:02:17,000
So here's the machine. If Penny pays it back, the shop keeps the fees.

36
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:22,110
If she doesn't, the shop keeps the guitar and sells it for much more than it lent.

37
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:29,590
In Oregon, for example, about two out of three pawn loans get redeemed, and the other third end up for sale.

38
00:02:30,199 --> 00:02:34,680
And the shop rarely gets burned on the resale.

39
00:02:34,979 --> 00:02:38,360
Before lending a cent, it checks what similar items actually sold for online.

40
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:41,020
And for gold, it doesn't even guess.

41
00:02:41,699 --> 00:02:48,530
It just weighs it. Gold has a price per gram, so the shop knows the minimum it can get, almost to the dollar.

42
00:02:49,370 --> 00:02:51,069
Either way, the house wins.

43
00:02:51,870 --> 00:02:53,810
And this isn't just a few corner shops.

44
00:02:54,539 --> 00:03:04,719
The biggest pawn company, FirstCash, is publicly traded, with more than three thousand three hundred stores, and the people they serve are usually the ones banks turned away.

45
00:03:05,509 --> 00:03:10,000
Because here's what a pawn shop really sells:

46
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:12,800
a yes, in ten minutes, with no paperwork and no awkward questions.

47
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:15,189
That convenience is the product.

48
00:03:15,939 --> 00:03:17,539
And convenience is expensive.

49
00:03:18,360 --> 00:03:20,110
So, is pawning always bad?

50
00:03:20,849 --> 00:03:27,189
Not necessarily. Pawn loans don't need a credit check, and if you can't pay, you lose the item, but your credit score isn't touched.

51
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:29,210
But if you ever do it:

52
00:03:30,050 --> 00:03:32,349
One: know the monthly fee before you sign.

53
00:03:32,919 --> 00:03:35,020
Two:

54
00:03:35,689 --> 00:03:37,810
borrow the least you can. Three:

55
00:03:38,590 --> 00:03:42,150
pay it off in the first cycle if you possibly can. Every extra month is a coin in that jar.

56
00:03:42,789 --> 00:03:45,169
Pawn shops aren't really in the buying and selling business.

57
00:03:46,189 --> 00:03:48,900
They're in the time business.

58
00:03:49,560 --> 00:03:53,909
They profit from how long it takes you to pay them back.

59
00:03:54,590 --> 00:03:56,719
And they're not the only ones playing that game.

60
00:03:57,520 --> 00:04:01,009
Your credit card company plays it too, and they're even better at it.

61
00:04:01,780 --> 00:04:03,639
That's next, on Money Mechanics.

